TY  - JOUR
AU  - Blacha, S
AU  - Anderson, IA
AU  - Mar, RA
TI  - Tension in Attention: Hypervigilance Helps Explain Why Marginalization Leads to Doomscrolling
T2  - PSYCHOLOGY OF POPULAR MEDIA
AB  - The use of social media has become increasingly prevalent and this has yielded some benefits, such as providing an alternative to traditional news sources and facilitating interpersonal interactions. However, some uses can also bring adverse effects such as doomscrolling: the persistent, habitual, consumption of negative news through scrolling endless, algorithmically sorted feeds. Doomscrolling is known to contribute to anxiety, and past studies suggest that this anxiety occurs more often in members of marginalized groups. We hypothesize that this is due to vigilance for potential discrimination among the marginalized, consistent with Minority Stress Theory. In a preregistered study, we recruited 677 university students who rated themselves on measures of doomscrolling, hypervigilance, and marginalization. We then investigated the degree to which identification with membership in a marginalized group predicts doomscrolling, and whether hypervigilance acts as a mediator for this association. In our data, marginalization was indeed a predictor of doomscrolling, and hypervigilance partially mediated this relationship. Finally, we investigated two other hypotheses: whether marginalized individuals also experience lower life satisfaction, or greater social connectedness, as a function of doomscrolling, compared to those less marginalized. In both cases, marginalization was not a moderator of these effects. Our study presents evidence that hypervigilance motivates doomscrolling for members of marginalized groups, suggesting that interventions seeking to address doomscrolling among the marginalized should combat underlying fears of discrimination.
