TI  - How is parental phubbing associated with online trolling among early adolescents? The mediating role of alexithymia and the moderating role of online disinhibition
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - Online trolling, an emerging and rapidly developing form of anti-social behavior characterized by inciting and disrupting online activities to cause controversy and conflict, has a detrimental impact on both online participants and the online environment. This study aimed to investigate the association between parental phubbing (parents ignoring children by focusing on their mobile phones) and online trolling among early adolescents. Additionally, it explored the mediating role of alexithymia (difficulty in identifying and expressing emotions) and the moderating role of online disinhibition (reduced self-regulation in the online environment) in this relationship. A total of 1073 Chinese early adolescents were recruited to complete scales assessing parental phubbing, alexithymia, online disinhibition, and online trolling. After controlling for gender and age, the results showed that parental phubbing was positively correlated with online trolling in Chinese early adolescents, and alexithymia mediated this relationship. Furthermore, online disinhibition moderated the relationship between parental phubbing and online trolling, as well as the relationship between alexithymia and online trolling. Specifically, both associations strengthened as the online disinhibition of early adolescents increased. Parents should reflect on negative interaction patterns with early adolescents to increase positive attention and reduce phubbing. Interventions focusing on alexithymia or online disinhibition may help reduce online trolling among early adolescents.
