TI  - Correlates of problematic smartphone use among Chinese adolescents during COVID-19
T2  - CHILDREN AND YOUTH SERVICES REVIEW
AB  - Objectives: During the COVID-19 pandemic, adolescents experienced disruptions to their normal routines and faced significant uncertainty that may have increased psychological distress. According to the compensatory internet use theory, smartphone use may alleviate psychological stress, but overreliance on smartphones to cope with stress might lead to smartphone addiction. Hence, we investigated the prevalence of problematic smartphone use (PSU) during COVID-19 among Macao Chinese adolescents, associated correlates, and moderation effects of Fear of Missing Out on relations between psychological distress and PSU severity. Methods: 1,735 Macao secondary school students completed an online questionnaire between June 2020 and July 2020, which included the Smartphone Addiction Scale short version (SAS-SV), Depression, Anxiety and Stress Scale-21 (DASS-21), Fear of missing out (FoMO) Scale, demographic information, and hours of smartphone usage during COVID-19. Data were analyzed using hierarchical multiple regression. Results: The estimated prevalence of PSU during COVID-19 was 50% among male adolescents and 53% among females. Psychological distress, longer smartphone usage time during COVID-19, and greater levels of FoMO were significantly associated with greater PSU severity. FoMO negatively moderated the association between psychological distress and PSU severity. Conclusion: This study demonstrated a high prevalence of PSU among Macao Chinese adolescents during the COVID-19 pandemic. FoMO was a key modifier between psychological distress and PSU severity and should be further investigated as an important treatment target to reduce PSU severity.
