AU  - Latifi, Q
AU  - Brown, T
TI  - User-Avatar Bond and psychological distress profiling: longitudinal impacts on Internet Gaming Disorder
T2  - CLINICAL PSYCHOLOGIST
AB  - ObjectiveThis study investigates the role of the User-Avatar Bond and psychological distress as risk factors for Internet Gaming Disorder over time.MethodA sample of 458 online gamers (mean age = 28.7, SD = 10.2) participated in surveys administered across two waves, six months apart. Using Latent Profile Analysis, the study identified subgroups based on User-Avatar Bond and psychological distress (depression, anxiety, and stress). ANOVA analyses assessed differences in Internet Gaming Disorder symptoms among these subgroups.ResultsFour distinct gamer profiles emerged: Compensated Gamers, Disengaged Gamers, High Distress, Engaged Gamers, and Immersed Gamers. Profiles with high psychological distress and intense avatar identification showed significantly higher symptoms of Internet Gaming Disorder.ConclusionsThe findings underscore the importance of tailored interventions that address both psychological distress and levels of engagement with digital avatars in reducing Internet Gaming Disorder risk. Gamified e-health tools using personalised digital characters may promote balanced avatar engagement.
