TI  - Longitudinal analysis of depressive symptoms among LGBTQ youth at a social media-free camp
T2  - JOURNAL OF GAY & LESBIAN MENTAL HEALTH
AB  - Introduction: Frequent social media use (SMU) is associated with elevated depressive symptoms (DS) for youth, but the relationship over time is unclear.
   Method: LGBTQ youth ages 12-18 (N= 214) were surveyed before and after attending a social media-free summer camp.
   Results: Pre-camp, SMU was not associated with DS. However, the relationship between pre- and post-camp DS was moderated by pre-camp SMU (b= -.03,p= .02). For youth with lower SMU, pre-camp DS more strongly predicted post-camp DS.
   Conclusion: Findings highlight the influence of a "social media break" in an affirming environment on LGBTQ youth mental health.
