AU  - Dursun, OB
TI  - Anxiety, depression and smartphone use in adolescents: the mediating roles of FoMO, phubbing and social exclusion
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - The association between adolescents' problematic smartphone use (PSU) and emotional symptoms, together with underlying socio-cognitive mechanisms, has received increasing research attention. The present study examined the relationships among anxiety, depression, fear of missing out (FoMO), phubbing, social exclusion, and objectively recorded screen time in adolescents. In this study, screen time was conceptualized as an objective behavioral indicator of digital engagement rather than a direct operationalization of problematic smartphone use. The sample consisted of 303 adolescents aged 12-18 years. Anxiety and depression symptoms were assessed using the Revised Child Anxiety and Depression Scale (RCADS), while FoMO, phubbing, and ostracism were measured using self-report questionnaires. Weekly screen time was obtained from device-based screen time records. Associations among variables were analyzed using structural equation modeling. The findings indicated that anxiety was indirectly associated with higher screen time through FoMO and phubbing. Depression showed both positive and negative indirect associations with screen time through socio-cognitive pathways, alongside a positive direct association. Overall, depressive symptoms were associated with higher total screen time. These results suggest that anxiety and depression are differentially related to adolescents' screen use, with FoMO and phubbing emerging as key socio-cognitive processes. From a conceptual perspective, these emotional and cognitive mechanisms may help explain patterns of excessive digital engagement during adolescence.
