AU  - de Vaate, NAJDB
TI  - Content-specific social media use: how a content-specific approach can further unravel the effects of social media use on well-being and body image
T2  - TIJDSCHRIFT VOOR COMMUNICATIEWETENSCHAP
AB  - Social media are often portrayed in the media as key determinants of disturbed wellbeing or body image among adolescents. Despite several studies confirm ing this negative relationship, the results are not always unequivocal. As a result, there is currently a scientific as well a societal debate about the possible effects of social media use on adolescents' wellbeing and body image. To further unravel the possible impact of social media use on wellbeing and body image, there is an explicit need to look more closely at the content on social media. Such as, what do young people see when they are on social media, and what do they post? What impact does that have on their wellbeing and body image? And for which adolescents are the potential effects more harmful or beneficial? The overarching goal of this doctoral research was to examine how adolescents use social media and if, when, and how it affects their wellbeing and body image. This article summarized studies that examined how content specific social media use is related to wellbeing and body image among adolescents.
