AU  - Casañas, R
AU  - Martín, J
AU  - Encuentra, EH
TI  - Mental health literacy on social media among secondary school students in Barcelona
T2  - MENTAL HEALTH & PREVENTION
AB  - Objective: This qualitative study explores how adolescents understand and navigate mental health literacy (MHL) within digital environments. Methods: Twenty-eight semi-structured interviews were conducted with secondary school students (aged 12-16) in Barcelona. The findings are reported following the Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Research (COREQ) checklist. Results: Thematic analysis revealed that adolescents primarily define mental health as subjective well-being and acknowledge its importance. MHL was conceptualized as a practical and necessary competence for self-help and supporting others. Despite intensive use of platforms such as TikTok, Instagram, and WhatsApp, most adolescents do not actively seek mental health information online, citing concerns about credibility and a preference for faceto-face support from trusted adults or professionals. When using digital platforms, adolescents tend to rely on unsophisticated search strategies, primarily via Google, and value content from verified sources. Participants expressed a desire for short, engaging, solution-focused content, particularly in video or infographic format, authored by experts or individuals with lived experience. Conclusions: The findings highlight a gap between adolescents' digital engagement and their ability to locate reliable mental health information. Promoting MHL via youth-centered, credible, and well-designed digital content-particularly on favored platforms like TikTok and Instagram-could enhance accessibility and help-seeking behaviors. This study underscores the need for integrated school and digital strategies to foster adolescent mental health literacy.
