TY  - JOUR
AU  - Hasegawa, C
AU  - Fukuda, K
TI  - Why do adolescents use smartphones at the expense of sleep? Patterns of motives for smartphone use and their associations with sleep and mental health
T2  - INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF ADOLESCENCE AND YOUTH
AB  - The aim of this study was to identify patterns of motives underlying adolescents' smartphone use in bed and examine associations with sleep and mental health. A total of 820 adolescents aged 12-18 reported sleep habits, six motives for smartphone use in bed, and completed the Pediatric Daytime Sleepiness Scale, Pre-Sleep Arousal Scale, Generalized Anxiety Disorder-7, and Compulsive Internet Use Scale. Overall, 61.7% reported smartphone use in bed. Four motive-based clusters were identified: diverse motives (Cluster 1), diverse motives excluding distraction (Cluster 2), habitual use (Cluster 3), and purposeful use (Cluster 4). Compared with those in Cluster 4, adolescents in Clusters 1-3 showed greater social jetlag and an evening chronotype. Cluster 1 also showed higher daytime sleepiness, pre-sleep cognitive arousal, anxiety symptoms, and internet addiction. These findings indicate that motives for smartphone use in bed are differentially associated with sleep and mental health among adolescents.
