TI  - Network analysis of problematic internet use, negative emotions, and somatic symptoms among Chinese adolescents
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - Problematic Internet Use (PIU) has emerged as a pressing public health issue in adolescent populations, frequently associated with adverse emotional states and somatic manifestations. Nevertheless, the connections among these symptom clusters remain poorly elucidated. Network analysis revealed interconnections among PIU, negative emotions, and somatic symptoms in 5,221 Chinese adolescents (aged 10-19) from Shandong Province. Standardized psychometric instruments were administered, including PIU (IAT), depression (CDI), anxiety (MASC), and somatic complaints (PHQ-13). Gaussian Graphical Models (GGMs) identified core and bridge symptoms, with sex differences assessed via Network Comparison Test (NCT). Based on the IAT and the cut-off score used in this study, 11.91% of participants were screened as being at risk for PIU. "Physical symptoms" of anxiety (MASC1) showed the highest centrality metrics, serving as both a hub and bridge within the symptom network. NCT showed no sex differences in global strength (p = 0.058) but significant structural divergence (p = 0.009). In males, the strongest network connection was observed between "ineffectiveness" and "feeling that your heart is thumping or beating fast"; whereas in females, the most robust association was found between "withdrawal and social problems" and "nausea, flatulence or indigestion". This study pioneers the application of network analysis to elucidate symptom-level associations between PIU, negative emotions, and somatic symptoms in adolescents. Anxiety-related physical symptoms emerged as central network nodes, with significant gender differences in bridge symptoms. These insights offer novel directions for targeted interventions, emphasizing the need for symptom-focused and gender-sensitive treatment strategies for PIU-affected youth.
